(project tuna-storage-law
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: the values law - content-addressed values
      are the primitive; mutable tree hierarchies are FORESWORN.
      This chapter promines PP's one-tree lesson into tuna book law
      (what the 2026-09-15 STORAGE SUBSTRATE WATCH note and the
      tuna2 exploratory draft carried as provenance, now chapter-
      form as the operator asked). Provenance: PP's one-tree bet
      landed HALFWAY and the halfway state was the expensive one -
      reads flipped, writes never retired their relational truth,
      and the mutable shared-hierarchy substrate collected seven
      subsystems (path grammar, label codec, shadow-mirror SQL
      triggers, branch override walks, sibling order, version
      shadows, retention) that were all paid for and none
      load-bearing; the trees survived only as a UI projection.
      The one-line memorial, kept verbatim from the autopsy:
      MUTABLE TREE STORAGE IS A SUBSYSTEM YOU INHERIT FOREVER;
      IMMUTABLE CONTENT-ADDRESSED VALUES ARE A PRIMITIVE YOU PAY
      FOR ONCE. Tuna's trees are values where PP's were state -
      this chapter keeps them that.")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section values-law
  (doc "The storage doctrine, as numbered laws over what the
        migrations already ship - this chapter codifies shipped
        reality, it does not request new storage.")
  (status implemented)
  (subsection one-tree-memorial
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      WHAT PP PAID, as the standing example: 009_tree.sql at 474
      lines, tree_store.ml at 2,578 lines, ~89 tree-touching
      commits, 9 dedicated tree test suites, 8 migrations - a
      perpetual dual-run state where the substrate never became
      the truth and the truth never reverted the substrate. The
      half-migration is the worst place to land. PP's one-tree
      chapter stays archived there with status partial forever,
      honest about it; tuna learned from the corpse. THE TEST for
      any future storage idea: does it introduce NAME-LEVEL MUTABLE
      STRUCTURE, or VALUE-LEVEL IMMUTABLE CONTENT? Values pass.
      Mutable hierarchies of named things do not. |))))
  (subsection laws
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      1. VALUES ARE THE PRIMITIVE. programs (0001: hash over
         ternary), tree_values (0006), byte_values (0008): all
         content-addressed, all sha256 over exact stored payload,
         lowercase hex, one address law across kinds. The hash IS
         identity: copying is aliasing, dedup is INSERT-side and
         free, equality is address comparison.
      2. NAMES ARE REFS, NOT VALUES. Every name-bearing surface is
         a pointer row: repl_dict (0004: identity, name -> ternary
         ref), tree_paths owners, the stdlib's sabralib dictionary
         entries, grant rows. Refs are mutable ROWS pointing at
         immutable content; ref ownership is authz (an identity's
         rows are its own), never courtesy. Names live at the rim
         only - domain rule 7's deeper claim, discovered by trying
         to violate it: nothing below the calculus ever learns
         what a name is.
      3. PATHS ARE DERIVED, NEVER TRUTH. tree_paths (0005) is its
         own drafting comment: a DERIVED index, value_hash in,
         path->hash out. The write truth is the chained effect
         log; rewind = fold of the log; a derived index earns its
         place exactly when it is REBUILDABLE from truth - an
         index that cannot be rebuilt from values + ops is not an
         index, it is a second truth, and second truths are what
         F9 exists to reject.
      4. MUTABLE HIERARCHIES ARE FORSWORN. No user-owned mutable
         path contexts; no path grammars or label codecs; no
         sibling order; no inheritance or override walks owned by
         storage; no version shadows; no per-path retention
         policies; no generations. If a feature seems to need one
         of these, the need goes to FINDINGS and the design goes
         through refs+values first - a book change amends THIS
         chapter in the open or it does not happen.
      5. CONTENT ROWS NEVER UPDATE IN PLACE. Content-addressed
         rows are write-once; versioning is a fresh hash + fresh
         row (patch CAS, op shadowing); death is a tombstone
         (journal.borg retention-GC), never a mutation; PII
         redaction is an explicit chain break, priced as one.
      6. THE AUDIT RITUAL IS LAW. Every future migration that
         introduces a table preferring a path or parent pointer
         over a hash answers the review question: why is this not
         a ref row over content-addressed values plus a derived
         index? "It is convenient" is not an answer; the seven
         subsystems of the one-tree corpse were convenient too. |))))
  (subsection pre-registered-failure
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      F9 SUBSTRATE REGRESSION. TRIGGER: any future migration, PR,
      or make-pass introduces mutable path-hierarchy storage truth
      (user-owned mutable path contexts; in-place updates of
      content rows; a second write-truth beside the effect log).
      THEN: reject as a spec violation - not judgment, doctrine:
      this chapter is the standing rejection written in advance.
      The failure is pre-registered so that no future loop can
      feign ambiguity between ref-rows (legal, law 2 + refs are
      how sharing works) and hierarchy-state (illegal, law 4). If
      a real product demand appears to require hierarchy, the
      FINDING is recorded with the demand as evidence and the law
      is amended in this book by an owner-dated change FIRST -
      the cruft otherwise repeats PP's years. |))))))
